BUGS
====

mediathek-list: HTTP(S) client never actually downloaded/parsed the Filmliste [FIXED]
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Turned out to be five separate, independent bugs stacked on top of each
other. Each one only became visible once the previous one was fixed, since
each hid the next behind an early abort or an infinite loop. All five are
now fixed; `./mediathek-list <keyword>` downloads the ~75 MB compressed
Filmliste over HTTPS, decompresses it, filters it, prints matching entries,
prints "Processed N entries." and exits.

1. mediathek-list.c: parse_mediathek_list() treated any -1 return from
   buffer_get_token() as fatal, without checking whether it was just EAGAIN
   (routine on a non-blocking socket, happens on effectively every call once
   the first TCP read's worth of data is consumed). This closed the input
   buffer after the very first read.
   Fix: only treat ret==-1 as fatal when errno isn't EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK.

2. lib/buffer/buffer_lzma.c: buffer_lzmaread_op() treated buffer_feed()'s
   return value of -1 (EAGAIN, "no new compressed bytes yet") the same as a
   nonzero success and fell through to run lzma_code() with avail_in==0,
   which produces a spurious ret==0 — indistinguishable from real
   end-of-stream. This made an ordinary "not ready yet" look like the input
   had legitimately closed.
   Fix: return immediately (propagating -1 or 0 unchanged) whenever
   buffer_feed() returns <= 0, instead of continuing into lzma_code().

3. lib/http/http_read.c: computed the "was this just EAGAIN?" check using
   response->err, which is a stale/mostly-unset field for TLS reads (it's
   only populated by http_socket_read() on the narrow
   TLS_ERR_SYSCALL/non-TLS path, never on ordinary SSL_ERROR_WANT_READ).
   The correct, always-populated field is h->err. Because of this, the very
   first ordinary WANT_READ during body transfer got misclassified as
   HTTP_STATUS_ERROR, and the *next* raw socket read call then saw that
   already-set ERROR status and permanently called io_dontwantread() /
   io_dontwantwrite() on the socket — which is why the process hung forever
   in `poll([], 0, 1000)` with an empty fd set and no further syscalls.
   Fix: use h->err (not response->err) for both the status-transition check
   and the errno set on early return.

4. lib/http/http_canread.c: the header-parsing loop set
   response->transfer = HTTP_TRANSFER_CHUNKED as an unconditional `else`
   fallback for *any* header line that wasn't Content-Type: multipart or
   Content-Length — i.e. every other header (Date, Server, Connection,
   ETag, ...). Since Content-Length is rarely the last header in a real
   response, this silently overwrote a correct HTTP_TRANSFER_LENGTH
   designation (and zeroed content_length) as soon as one more header line
   was parsed. The body was then fed through the chunked-transfer decoder,
   which looks for a hex chunk-size line + CRLF in raw compressed binary
   data that will never contain one — http_response_read() returned 0
   forever while buffered bytes sat unconsumed, spinning at 100% CPU with
   zero syscalls.
   Fix: only set CHUNKED when the header is actually
   "Transfer-Encoding: chunked", not as a catch-all default.

5. lib/http/http_response_read.c: the HTTP_TRANSFER_LENGTH case (ordinary
   Content-Length body, which is what this server actually sends) never
   set `ret` at all — it stayed at its initial 0 forever, regardless of how
   much body data was buffered and available. Any caller that keeps looping
   while there's still buffered input and `ret>=0` (http_read_internal's own
   HTTP_RECV_DATA case, and http_read()'s outer retry loop) span forever
   making zero progress once this case was reached.
   Fix: compute `ret = MIN(content_length - data_pos, buffer_LEN(in))` (the
   same pattern already used by the CHUNKED case) and set
   HTTP_STATUS_FINISH once fully consumed.

6. lib/http/http_read.c: http_read()'s own outer "again:" retry loop looped
   whenever `buffer_LEN(in)` (raw buffered bytes) was nonzero, without ever
   checking whether its *own* destination buffer (n) still had room. Once
   the destination was fully written in one internal call, every further
   iteration called http_read_internal() with n==0, which correctly copies
   zero bytes every time but never breaks the outer loop either — an
   infinite loop making zero progress, independent of bug #3 above, that
   also happened to hide bug #5's return-value bug for a while.
   Fix: only loop again when the last internal call made forward progress
   and the destination buffer isn't already full
   (`got > 0 && (size_t)got < n && buffer_LEN(in)`); accumulate `ret`
   properly across iterations instead of overwriting it.

7. mediathek-list.c: parse_mediathek_list()'s inner `for(;;)` loop (the one
   scanning one byte at a time past a closing `"],` to find the next
   record) never broke when buffer_get() returned <= 0. Once the transfer
   legitimately reached true end-of-stream, this spun forever re-requesting
   the same unavailable byte at 100% CPU with zero syscalls, right after
   the download had actually finished successfully (all entries already
   parsed and written) — the tool would never print the final summary or
   exit.
   Fix: break out of the inner loop as soon as buffer_get() returns <= 0.

8. mediathek-list.c: main()'s network-mode event loop (the non `-f` path)
   had no exit condition at all tied to the transfer completing — it just
   called io_waituntil2(1000) forever even after all data had been
   downloaded, decompressed and parsed. This isn't a spin (it blocks
   correctly in poll()), but the process would never terminate or print
   "Processed N entries.".
   Fix: break out of the outer for(;;) once the request has been sent and
   buffer_EOF(&in) is true (mirrors the termination check already used by
   the `-f <file>` code path).

Verified: `./mediathek-list test` now completes in one run, ending with
`Processed 25355 entries.` and well-formed JSON-ish output on stdout.

Unrelated observation, not fixed (cosmetic/noisy, not a functional bug):
http_socket_read() in lib/http/http_socket.c has an unconditional
`#if 1 // def DEBUG_OUTPUT` block that writes "recv() = ..." to stderr on
every single read, plus a "verify callback ok=0" debug line elsewhere from
the TLS verify callback — these look like leftover debugging output that
should be gated behind DEBUG_HTTP rather than always-on.
